      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section class="section" id="profit-impact">
        <h2>4. Impact on Manufacturing Profits</h2>
        <p>
          For an industrial manufacturing startup, every gram of pigment represents a significant cost. If your dispersion process is inefficient, you are essentially "burying" expensive pigment within agglomerates where they cannot contribute to the color. This means you have to use more pigment to reach the same opacity, directly cutting into your industry profits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Furthermore, poor dispersion leads to batch inconsistency. If one batch is better dispersed than the next, the colors will not match, leading to expensive returns or rework. High-quality dispersants ensure that your "fineness of grind"—measured by a Hegman gauge—is reached quickly, saving electricity and wear-and-tear on your machinery. Efficiency in the lab translates directly to success in the market.
        </p>

        <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
          <li><strong>Pigment Efficiency:</strong> Get up to 30% more color strength from the same amount of pigment.</li>
          <li><strong>Viscosity Stability:</strong> Prevent thinning or thickening of paint during storage.</li>
          <li><strong>Gloss Retention:</strong> Smaller, well-dispersed particles reflect light more uniformly.</li>
          <li><strong>Reduced Grind Time:</strong> Spend less time at the mixer, increasing your daily production capacity.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 (Troubleshooting with 4-line introductory paragraph) -->
      <section class="section" id="troubleshooting">
        <h2>5. Troubleshooting Common Failures</h2>
        <p>
          In colloidal science, the stability of a paint system is constantly threatened by thermal and mechanical perturbations that drive particles toward flocculation or hard sedimentation. Applying classical DLVO theory reveals that the net interaction energy between two pigment particles is a balance between van der Waals attractive forces and electrostatic or steric repulsive barriers. If the local Zeta potential of the particles drops below a critical threshold (typically +/- 30 millivolts in water-based systems), or if the steric polymer brush becomes desolvated, the attractive forces will dominate, leading to rapid clumping. Understanding these microscopic thermodynamics is crucial for process engineers trying to isolate the root cause of viscosity spikes, color drift, or heavy paint settling inside industrial warehouses.
        </p>

        <div class="image-slot">
          <img 
            src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/AGBzPMBJDQiwDGny/stability-comparison-rxX335JOjJf6AzKP.png" 
            alt="Microscopic comparison of stable pigment dispersion with individual, well-spaced particles versus a flocculated paint system with clustered agglomerates" 
            loading="lazy" 
          />
        </div>
        
        <p>
          Flocculation is the nightmare of every paint chemist. It occurs when particles that were once dispersed start to clump back together into "flocs." Unlike the original hard agglomerates, flocs can sometimes be broken with a stir, but they will reform the moment the paint is applied to a wall, causing color shifts and poor film integrity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sedimentation, or "hard settling," is the other major hurdle. This happens when the dispersant fails to keep heavy pigment particles suspended. Over time, these particles sink and form a concrete-like layer at the bottom of the tin that no amount of shaking can fix. Solving these issues requires a deep dive into the Zeta potential of the particles and the compatibility of the dispersant with the resin system.
        </p>

