        <p>
          Operating a successful paint and coatings factory requires maintaining a highly technical and disciplined culture in both the research laboratory and the production floor. Chemical startups must combine process engineering rigor, raw material quality control, and workplace safety compliance to remain highly competitive and regulatory-safe. The following frequently asked questions address standard operational challenges, testing methodologies, and additive selection criteria critical for daily manufacturing success. These technical answers provide actionable guidance to troubleshoot your plant floor operations and maximize batch consistency.
        </p>

        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. Can I use the same dispersant for water-based and solvent-based paints?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Generally, no. Water-based systems require hydrophilic anchors and often rely on electrostatic repulsion, while solvent-based systems require lipophilic chains and rely heavily on steric stabilization. Using the wrong type will cause the pigment to "shock" and clump immediately.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. How do I know if my paint is properly dispersed?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              The industry standard is a Hegman Gauge test. A reading of 7 or 8 indicates a very fine dispersion suitable for high-gloss finishes, while a 4 or 5 might be acceptable for matte primers.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. Why is my paint color different after it has been stored for a month?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              This is likely due to flocculation or "floating" of pigments. If the dispersant isn't strong enough, different pigments (like white and blue) will separate, causing a color shift. This is known as the "rub-up" test failure.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Do dispersants affect the drying time of the paint?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              In some cases, yes. Excessive amounts of certain surfactant-based dispersants can slow down drying or affect the final water resistance of the paint film. Balance is critical.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. Are polymeric dispersants better than standard surfactants?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              For high-performance applications, yes. Polymeric dispersants provide much better long-term stability and are less likely to migrate out of the paint film over time compared to small-molecule surfactants.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. How can Global Formulation help my paint business?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              We provide end-to-end technical support, from selecting the specific chemical additives for your local climate to setting up industrial-scale production lines and quality control protocols.
            </p>
          </div>

